To calculate the entropy change (ΔS) for the reaction, we use the formula:  
\[ \Delta S = \sum \text{(Mass_i} \times \text{Osm}_i \times \text{L}_i) - \sum \text{(Mass_i} \times \text{Osm}_i \cdot \text{L}_i) \]  
Where \( \text{M}_i \) is the molar intensity of the \( i \)-th molecule, \( \text{O}_i \) is the molar osmotic index of the \( i \)-th molecule, and \( \text{L}_i \) is the molar load of the \( i \)-th molecule.  
For a given reaction, the entropy change is calculated as:  
\[ \Delta S = \sum \text{Osm} \times \text{L} - \sum \text{Osm} \times \text{L} \]  
Here, the molar susceptities (or molar loads) are given in the problem as molar osmotic indices.  

The formula for the entropy change is:  
\[ \Delta S = \sum \text{L} \times \text{O} - \sum \text{L} \text{O} \]  
where \( \text{L} = \text{L}_i \) and \( \text{O} = \text{O}_i \).  

For the given reaction:  
\[ 2 \text{PbS(s)} + 3 \text{O}_2(g) \rightarrow 2 \text{PbO(s)} + 2 \text{SO}_2(g) \]  
\[ \text{L}_1 = 2 \times 91.2, \quad \text{O}_1 = 99.2 \]  
\[ \text{L}_2 = 3 \times 205.1, \quad \text{O}_2 = 205.1 \]  
\( \text{L}_3 = 2 \times 66.5, \quad \text{O}_3 = 66.5 \)  
\( \text{L}_4 = 2 \times 248.2, \quad \text{O}_4 = 248.2 \)  

Substituting the given molar osmotic indices (which are the molar susceptities):  
\[ \Delta S = (2 \times 91.2) + (3 \times 205.1) - (2 \times 66.5) - (2 \times 248.2) \]  
\[ \Delta S = 182.4 + 615.3 - 133 - 496.4 \]  
\[ \Delta S = 797.7 - 629.4 \]  
\[ \Delta S = 168.3 \]  
