To calculate the standard hydrostatic energy change (ΔH) for the reaction, we use the formula:

ΔH = Σ(ΔH_i_i) (out) - Σ(ΔH_i_i) (out)

Where:
- ΔH_i_i_i is the heat of ionizationization (H_o) of each reactant and effluent.

In this case, the reaction is:

CaO(s) + H2O(l) → Ca(OH)_2(s)

The known masses of the hydrogen of phosphorus are:
- H_o(CaO) = -635.1 kJ/mol
- H_o(H_2O) = -285.8 kJ/mol
- H_2O(Ca(OH)_2) = -986.2 kJ/mol

Using the formula, we get:

ΔH = H_o(Ca(OH)_2) - (H_o(CoA) + H_o(H_2O))
ΔH = -986.2 kg/mol - (-635.1 kJ/mol - 285.8 kJ/mol)
ΔH = -986.2 kg/mol + 635.1 kJ/mol + 285.8 kJ/mol
ΔH = -106.3 kJ/mol

The standard hydrostatic energy change for the reaction between calcium oxide and water to form calcium hydroxide is $\boxed{-106.3 \text{ kg/mol}}$.
