<GROUND_TRUTH>
Absolute asymmetric synthesis refers to the preparation of optically active compounds from achiral (optically inactive) starting materials without the use of any chiral reagent, catalyst, or auxiliary. Instead, it relies on a physically dissymmetric influence, such as circularly polarized light, to induce chirality in the product. This differs from other types of asymmetric synthesis, which typically require a chiral agent (e.g., a chiral catalyst or substrate) to achieve enantioselectivity.  

