","The equation of the tangent line to the graph of a differentiable function \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \) is given by:  
\[
f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)
\]  
This equation is derived from the point-slope form of a line, which is \( y - y_1 = m(x - x_1) \), where \( (x_1, y_1) \) is the point and \( m \) is the slope. Here, \( (x_1, y_1) = (a, f(a)) \).  

The slope \( m \) of the tangent line is the derivative of \( f \) at \( x = a \), which is \( f'(a) \).  

Substituting these values into the point-slope form, we get:  
\[
y - f(a) = f'(a)(x - a)
\]  
Rearranging this equation gives the desired form:  
\[
y = f(a) + f'(a)(x - a)
\]  

This equation represents the equation of the tangent line to the graph of \( f \) at the point \( (a, f(a)) \).  

