A jogger running at 9 km/hr alongside a railway track is 150 m ahead of the engine of a train running at 45 km/hr in the same direction. The train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. How long is the train in meters?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","First, we need to convert the speeds of the jogger and the train from km/hr to m/s.

The jogger's speed is 9 km/hr. To convert this to m/s, we multiply by (1000 m / 1 km) and (1 hr / 3600 s):
\[ 9 \text{ km/hr} = 9 \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{1 \text{ km}} \times \frac{1 \text{ hr}}{3600 \text{ s}} = \frac{9000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 2.5 \text{ m/s} \]

The train's speed is 42 km/hr. To convert this to m/s, we multiply by (1000 m / 1 km) and (1 hr / 3600 s):
\[ 45 \text{ km/hr} = 45 \times \frac{1000 \text{ m}}{1 \text{ km}} \times \frac{1 \text{ hr}}{3600 \text{ s}} = \frac{45000}{3600} \text{ m/s} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} \]

The train is 150 m ahead of the jogger, and the train takes 25 seconds to pass the jogger. This means that the train covers the distance of 150 m plus the length of the train in 25 seconds.

The relative speed of the train relative to the jogger is the difference in their speeds:
\[ \text{Relative speed} = 12.5 \text{ m/s} - 2.5 \text{ m s} = 10 \text{ m/s} \]

The train travels 150 m plus the length of the train in 25 seconds at a relative speed of 10 m/s. Therefore, the distance covered by the train relative to the jogger is:
\[ \text{Distance} = \text{Relative speed} \times \text{Time} \]
\[ \text{Distance} = 10 \text{ m/s} \times 25 \text{ s} = 250 \text{ m} \]

This distance is the sum of the initial distance between the train and the jogger (150 m) and the length of the train. Therefore, the length of the train is:
\[ \text{Length of the train} = \text{Distance} - \text{Initial distance} \]
\[ \text{Length of the train} = 250 \text{ m} - 150 \text{ m} = 100 \text{ m} \]

So, the length of the train is $\boxed{100}$  meters.",relevance,"# Task Description
