Using the trigonometric identities $\cos (180 ^{\circ} + \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, $\sin (\alpha + 360 ^{\circ}) = \sin \alpha$, $\sin (-\alpha - 180 ^{\circ}) = -\sin (\alpha + 180 ^{\circ}) = -\sin \alpha$, and $\cos (-180 ^{\circ} - \alpha) = -\cos \alpha$, we can simplify the expression as:
\[
\dfrac{-\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}{-\sin \alpha \cdot (-\cos \alpha)} = \frac{-\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}{\cos \alpha \cdot \sin \alpha}.
\]
