Given that the lateral surface area $A = \frac{3}{8}\pi$ and the slant height $\ell = 1$, we can solve for $r$:
\[
\frac{3}{8}\pi = \pi r \cdot 1 \implies r = \frac{3}{8}.
\]

The circumference of the base of the cone is $2\pi r = 2\pi \cdot \frac{3}{8} = \frac{3\pi}{4}$.

The circumference of the outer circle of the unfolded disk is $2\pi r_{\text{outer}}$, where $r_{\text{outer}}$ is the radius of the outer circle. This radius is the slant height of the cone, so $r_{\text{outer}} = 1$. The circumference of the outer circle is $2\pi \cdot 1 = 2\pi$.

The central angle $\theta$ of the unfolded diagram is the ratio of the circumference of the base to the circumference of the outer circle, multiplied by $360^\circ$ (or $2\pi$ radians):
\[
\theta = \frac{2\pi r}{\pi r_{\text{outer}}} \cdot 360^\circ = \frac{2\pi \cdot \frac{3}{8}}{2\pi} \cdot 360^\circ = \frac{\frac{3}{4}}{1} \cdot 360^\circ = \frac{3}{4} \cdot 360^\circ = 270^\circ.
\]

