","To show that the Dufroz-Froquz-Froelze (DF) method is stable for any \(\Delta t\), we analyze the stability condition for the discretized equation. The discretized equation is:
\[
\phi_j^{n+1} = \phi_j^{n-1} + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n - \phi_j^{n+1} - \phi_j^{n-1} + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
Rearranging the equation to solve for \(\phi_j^{n+1}\):
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_j^{n+1} = \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n \right) + \phi_{j-1}^{n+1}.
\]
Let \( \lambda = \frac{\Delta x}{\Delta t} \), and \( \lambda^2 = \frac{\Delta x^2}{(\Delta t)^2} \). The coefficients are:
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) = 1 + \frac{2\kappa}{\lambda^2}, \quad \frac{2\kappa (\Delta t)}{(\Delta x)^2} = \frac{2\kappa}{\lambda^2}.
\]
Rearranging the equation further gives:
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_j^{n+1} - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_{j+1}^n - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_{j-1}^n = \phi_{j+1}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n.
\]
This can be rewritten as:
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_j^{n+1} - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_{j+1}^n - \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \phi_{j-1}^n = \frac{\Delta t}{\Delta x}^2 \left( \frac{\Delta t}{\Delta x} \right)^2 \left( \phi_{j+1}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
\]
The discretized equation can be written as:
\[
\left( 1 + \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \right) \phi_j^{n+1} = \frac{2\kappa \Delta t}{(\Delta x)^2} \left( \phi_{j+1}^n + \phi_{j-1}^n \right).
