<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
To find the curl of the vector field \(\mathbf{V}(x, y)\), we first need to express it in terms of its components:
\[
\mathbf{V}(x, y) = \mathbf{V}_x(x, y) \mathbf{i} + \mathbf{V}_y(x, y) \mathbf{j}
\]
where \(\mathbf{V}_x(x, y)\) and \(\mathbf{V}_y(x,, y)\) are the \(x\)- and \(y\)-components of \(\mathbf{V}\), respectively.

The curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) is given by:
\[
\text{curl} \ \mathbf{V} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_x - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \mathbf{V}_y \mathbf{k}
\]
Substituting the components of \(\mathbf{V}\):
\[
\text{curl} \ \mathbf{V} = \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_x(x, y) - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \mathbf{V}_y(x, y) \mathbf{k}
\]
This represents the curl of \(\mathbf{V}\) in terms of its components. 

Thus, the curl of the vector field is:
\[
\boxed{\left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y} \mathbf{V}_x - \frac{\partial}{\partial x} \mathbf{V}_y \right) \mathbf{k}}
\]
